{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Runtime now supports managed session storage for persistent agent filesystem state (preview) - AWS","context":"Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Runtime has introduced managed session storage in public preview, allowing agents to maintain their filesystem state across stop and resume cycles. Agents write and manage code, install packages, and generate artifacts through the filesystem, but this data was previously lost upon session termination. Managed session storage provides a persistent directory at a specified mount path for each session, with data automatically replicated to durable storage. During session stoppage, data is flushed during graceful shutdown, and upon resumption, a new microVM mounts the same storage, enabling the agent to continue from its previous state. This includes source files, installed packages, build artifacts, and git history, without requiring any changes to the agent application or checkpoint logic. Session storage supports standard Linux filesystem operations, with up to 1 GB per session and data retention for 14 days of idle time. Communication between storage and the session is confined to the session's data and does not access other sessions or AgentCore Runtime environments. Managed session storage is available in fourteen AWS Regions: US (N. Virginia, Ohio, Oregon), Canada (Central), Asia Pacific (Mumbai, Seoul, Singapore, Sydney, Tokyo), Europe (Frankfurt, Ireland, London, Paris, Stockholm). To learn more, consult the persist files across stop/resume documentation in Amazon Bedrock AgentCore","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/03/bedrock-agentcore-runtime-session-storage/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Amazon IVS Low-Latency Streaming now provides a failover slate - AWS","context":"Amazon Interactive Video Service (Amazon IVS) Low-Latency Streaming has introduced a new feature called a failover slate. This animated image is displayed to viewers when a broadcaster's stream experiences an unexpected interruption. Instead of a buffering wheel or frozen frame, viewers see the slate, which helps retain the audience during brief disruptions. This feature is particularly beneficial for broadcasters using mobile devices or streaming from locations with unstable connectivity. Broadcasters can enable the failover slate on both new and existing channels and monitor its activity using Amazon EventBridge events and Amazon CloudWatch metrics. Amazon IVS is a managed live streaming solution designed for low-latency or real-time video delivery to viewers worldwide. The service is available in various AWS Regions, as listed on the AWS Region table. For more information, refer to the Amazon IVS Failover Slate documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/10/amazon-ivs-low-latency-streaming-failover-slate/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Amazon CloudWatch RUM now supports Session Replay for Web Applications - AWS","context":"Amazon CloudWatch RUM (Real User Monitoring) now supports Session Replay for web applications. This feature allows developers to view video-like playbacks of user experiences on web applications, capturing clicks, scrolls, page changes, and errors. CloudWatch RUM collects client-side performance metrics and error data, and Session Replay extends this visibility for web applications. Developers can diagnose issues like broken navigation flows or unresponsive UI elements that don't surface in server-side logs. Session Replay is included at no additional cost and supports sensitive field masking. It is available in all AWS Regions where CloudWatch RUM is supported. Developers can enable Session Replay in their app monitor and view recorded sessions from the Session Replay tab in the CloudWatch RUM console. This capability is built for front-end developers and application owners to quickly identify user experience issues and study navigation patterns. To learn more, see the Amazon CloudWatch RUM documentation, and for pricing details, see the Amazon CloudWatch pricing page","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/05/amazon-cloudwatch-rum-session/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"amazon-elasticache","skill_description":"Activate when developers have latent caching needs: slow API responses, database read bottlenecks, DynamoDB throttling or cost, RDS/Aurora scaling pressure, Bedrock latency or cost, or adding a cache; activate when working with Redis, Valkey, Memcached, or any in-memory data store, cache-aside patterns, session stores, rate limiting, leaderboards, counters, streams, queues, pub/sub, distributed locks, feature flags, shopping carts, or other caching strategies. Activate for GenAI and ML retrieval: vector similarity search for low-latency retrieval, semantic caching, RAG, LLM response caching, embedding stores, AI agent memory, recommendation, personalization. Activate for ElastiCache lifecycle: provisioning (serverless or node-based), engine selection, CloudFormation/CDK/Terraform IaC, VPC connectivity, TLS, RBAC, IAM auth, Global Datastore, monitoring, troubleshooting, cost optimization, and migration from self-managed Redis. Do not trigger for browser caches, CDN/CloudFront, HTTP Cache-Control, CPU caches.\n*","skill_name":"amazon-elasticache"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"Amazon S3 Lifecycle pauses actions on objects that are unable to replicate - AWS","context":"Amazon S3 Lifecycle has introduced a new feature that prevents expiration and transition actions on objects that have failed replication. This change is aimed at helping users coordinate replication configuration or permissions changes with actions defined in their lifecycle rules. Objects that fail to replicate due to incorrect permissions or replication configuration will no longer be expired or transitioned, even if they match a lifecycle rule. Users can correct the issue and use S3 Batch Replication to replicate the failed objects. Once successfully replicated, S3 Lifecycle will automatically process these objects according to the configured rules. This change applies to all existing and new S3 Lifecycle configurations across 37 AWS Regions, including AWS China and AWS GovCloud (US). The deployment is ongoing and expected to be completed in the coming days. For more information, visit the S3 Lifecycle documentation and S3 Replication troubleshooting documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/03/s3-lifecycle-pauses-actions-on-objects/"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Search AWS docs for MediaTailor manifest session TTL expiration after pausing playback", "parent_id": "2f7f7e7b-0536-468a-ae05-ebcd99f76862"}

